From: Simon Polack <spolack+git@mailbox.org>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: cavium: octeon_pip: prefetch what the completion frees

EXPERIMENTAL, showcase only, not for merging: it needs proper testing.
It changes the octeon_pip driver and depends on its PR (#26060).
The build does not apply it: it sits in target/linux/octeon/experimental/.

The completion prefetched the first line of the next completed skb. The
free reads its second line (head, truesize), the shared info (dataref,
the page pool flag) and the page struct (pp magic, refcount): all gone
from the L1 since the packet was received. Prefetch the two skb lines
two entries ahead and, from them, the shared info and the page struct
one entry ahead.

Measured on an EdgeRouter 4 (CN7030, 4 cores), octeon3 subtarget (CN63XXP1
off), octeon_pip driver, one boot per image, 64-byte single-flow floods
in kpps, on 2026-10-21.
Target: skb_release_data(), napi_pp_put_page(), the completion: 2.04 -> 0.86 L1 misses per
packet (flood1).
Step oct3B -> P1 (077-080 together): flood1 +3.8 %, fw4 NAT +1.4 %,
fw4+flowtable +4.1 %, GRE tcp1 +7.9 %.
This patch, extrapolated from its share of the misses the step removed (1.18 of 2.65):
flood1 ~+1.7 %, fw4 NAT ~+0.6 %, fw4+flowtable ~+1.8 %, GRE ~+1.6 %.
These gains are on top of an unpatched octeon3 kernel; the full set and the
split gain (up to +15 %) are in target/linux/octeon/experimental/README.md.

Signed-off-by: Simon Polack <spolack+git@mailbox.org>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/cavium/octeon/octeon_pip_tx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/cavium/octeon/octeon_pip_tx.c
@@ -231,6 +231,29 @@ static void opip_tx_doorbell(struct opip
  *
  * Return: the number of packets completed
  */
+/* The two lines of a completed entry's skb (or XDP frame) */
+static __always_inline void opip_tx_prefetch_entry(unsigned long entry)
+{
+	void *p = (void *)(entry & ~OPIP_TX_FLAGS);
+
+	prefetch(p);
+	prefetch(p + 128);
+}
+
+/*
+ * What the free of an skb reads beyond its own lines: the shared info and
+ * the page struct of its head. Its lines are in: prefetched a round ago.
+ */
+static __always_inline void opip_tx_prefetch_free(unsigned long entry)
+{
+	struct sk_buff *skb = opip_tx_entry_skb(entry);
+
+	if (skb) {
+		prefetch(skb_shinfo(skb));
+		prefetch(virt_to_page(skb->head));
+	}
+}
+
 static unsigned int opip_tx_complete(struct net_device *dev, unsigned int qid,
 				     struct opip_txq *q, int budget)
 {
@@ -256,6 +279,10 @@ static unsigned int opip_tx_complete(str
 
 	xdp_frame_bulk_init(&bq);
 	rcu_read_lock();
+	/* The first two entries: no round before them */
+	opip_tx_prefetch_entry(q->ring[head & (OPIP_TXQ_RING - 1)]);
+	if (n > 1)
+		opip_tx_prefetch_entry(q->ring[(head + 1) & (OPIP_TXQ_RING - 1)]);
 	while (n--) {
 		unsigned long entry = q->ring[head++ & (OPIP_TXQ_RING - 1)];
 		struct sk_buff *skb = opip_tx_entry_skb(entry);
@@ -263,10 +290,17 @@ static unsigned int opip_tx_complete(str
 
 		xdpf = opip_tx_entry_xdpf(entry);
 
-		/* The next one was sent long ago, its lines have gone */
+		/*
+		 * The next ones were sent long ago, their lines have gone:
+		 * the one after the next gets its own lines, the next what
+		 * its free reads
+		 */
+		if (n > 1)
+			opip_tx_prefetch_entry(q->ring[(head + 1) &
+						       (OPIP_TXQ_RING - 1)]);
 		if (n)
-			prefetch((void *)(q->ring[head & (OPIP_TXQ_RING - 1)] &
-					  ~OPIP_TX_FLAGS));
+			opip_tx_prefetch_free(q->ring[head &
+						      (OPIP_TXQ_RING - 1)]);
 		if (skb) {
 			if (entry & OPIP_TX_GATHER)
 				opip_fpa_free_nosync(*opip_tx_skb_list(skb),
